    "y2028": true,
    "stem": "<p>Titan and Rhea are moons of Saturn. Each moves in an approximately circular orbit. Titan has an orbital radius of $1.22 \\times 10^{9}$ m and an orbital period of 15.9 days. Rhea has an orbital radius of $5.27 \\times 10^{8}$ m.</p>",
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "A moon of mass $m$ orbits a planet of mass $M$ in a circle of radius $r$ with period $T$. Deduce that $T^2 = \\dfrac{4\\pi^2}{GM}r^3$.",
        "marks": 3,
        "markscheme": [
          "the gravitational force provides the centripetal force: $\\dfrac{GMm}{r^2} = \\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$ ✓",
          "with $v = \\dfrac{2\\pi r}{T}$ ✓",
          "so $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2} = \\dfrac{4\\pi^2 r}{T^2}$, which rearranges to $T^2 = \\dfrac{4\\pi^2}{GM}r^3$ ✓"
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "b",
        "question": "Determine the mass of Saturn.",
        "marks": 2,
        "markscheme": [
          "period in seconds: $T = 15.9$ days $= 1.37 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\text{s}$ ✓",
          "$M = \\dfrac{4\\pi^2 r^3}{GT^2} = 5.69 \\times 10^{26}\\ \\text{kg}$; allow ECF from (a) ✓"
        ],
        "numeric": {
          "answer": 5.69497e+26,
          "unit": "kg",
          "range": [
            5.52e+26,
            5.87e+26
          ],
          "mistakes": [
            {
              "value": 4.25127e+36,
              "feedback": "Convert the period to seconds before using T² = 4π²r³/(GM)."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "c",
        "question": "Calculate, in days, the orbital period of Rhea.",
        "marks": 2,
        "markscheme": [
          "$\\dfrac{T_{\\text{R}}^2}{r_{\\text{R}}^3} = \\dfrac{T_{\\text{T}}^2}{r_{\\text{T}}^3}$ (OR using the mass from (b)) ✓",
          "$T_{\\text{R}} = 15.9 \\times \\left(\\dfrac{5.27 \\times 10^{8}}{1.22 \\times 10^{9}}\\right)^{3/2} = 4.51$ days ✓"
        ],
        "numeric": {
          "answer": 4.51412,
          "unit": "day",
          "range": [
            4.37,
            4.65
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "d",
        "question": "Explain why the masses of the moons are not needed in (b) and (c).",
        "marks": 1,
        "markscheme": [
          "the moon's mass cancels (it appears on both sides of the force equation), so the orbital period does not depend on it ✓"
        ]
      }
